---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="fahrenheit-to-celsius">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="fahrenheit-to-celsius" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Subtract first, then scale</h2>
    <p>
      Fahrenheit to Celsius reverses both steps of the other direction, and the order matters:
    </p>
    <p><strong>&deg;C = (&deg;F &minus; 32) &times; 5/9</strong></p>
    <p>
      Subtract 32 to align the zero points, then multiply by 5/9, because a Fahrenheit degree is smaller.
      Dividing by 1.8 is the same operation and easier on a calculator. If you scale before you subtract,
      the answer is always 14.2 degrees too low.
    </p>

    <h2>Thermostats, weather and comfort</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">&deg;F</th><th scope="col">&deg;C</th><th scope="col">Feels like</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>-17.8</td><td>Severe cold</td></tr>
        <tr><td>32</td><td>0</td><td>Freezing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>50</td><td>10</td><td>Coat weather</td></tr>
        <tr><td>60</td><td>15.6</td><td>Cool spring day</td></tr>
        <tr><td>68</td><td>20</td><td>Standard room temperature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>72</td><td>22.2</td><td>The default US thermostat setting</td></tr>
        <tr><td>80</td><td>26.7</td><td>Warm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>90</td><td>32.2</td><td>Hot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>100</td><td>37.8</td><td>Very hot; above body temperature</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      For weather, the Fahrenheit scale fits human experience well: 0 to 100 &deg;F goes from "unbearably
      cold" to "unbearably hot" in most inhabited places, and the same span in Celsius is a narrow &minus;18
      to 38.
    </p>

    <h2>The mental shortcut</h2>
    <p>
      Subtract 30 and halve. 70 &rarr; 20 (true 21.1). 90 &rarr; 30 (true 32.2). 50 &rarr; 10 (true 10). It
      is a degree or two low in the middle of the range and exact at 50, which is enough to decide what to
      wear.
    </p>

    <h2>Cooking with a US recipe</h2>
    <p>
      American oven temperatures come in 25 &deg;F steps that give untidy Celsius numbers, so European
      editions round them:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>325 &deg;F = 162.8 &deg;C, written as 160 &deg;C (gas 3)</li>
      <li>350 &deg;F = 176.7 &deg;C, written as 180 &deg;C (gas 4)</li>
      <li>375 &deg;F = 190.6 &deg;C, written as 190 &deg;C (gas 5)</li>
      <li>400 &deg;F = 204.4 &deg;C, written as 200 &deg;C (gas 6)</li>
      <li>425 &deg;F = 218.3 &deg;C, written as 220 &deg;C (gas 7)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a fan oven, subtract another 20 &deg;C from those figures. The oven temperature converter on this
      site does the whole chain, including gas marks.
    </p>

    <h2>Fahrenheit&apos;s odd zero</h2>
    <p>
      In 1724 Daniel Fahrenheit set his zero at the temperature of an ice, water and ammonium chloride brine,
      the coldest thing he could reliably reproduce, and 96 at about human body temperature. The modern
      definition uses neither point; it is fixed by reference to the Celsius scale. This history is why the
      numbers do not line up neatly.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
